Algorithm 4: MST (G)

Input : a proximity graph G = (V, E) with each node u ∈ V having neighbors sorted in ascending order by their distances to u

Output : the MST of proximity graph G

1 T ← ∅; l[u] ← 1 for each u ∈ V;

2 assign u ∈ V to the set only contains itself;

3 while T contains less than |V| - 1 edges do

4    E_min[s] ← ∅ for each set s that contains any data point u ∈ V;

5    for each u ∈ V do

6    v ← the l[u]-th node in NG(u);

7    while l[u] ≤ |NG(u)| and u, v are in the same set do

8    l[u] ← l[u] + 1; v ← the l[u]-th node in NG(u);

9    if l[u] ≤ |NG(u)| then

10    s ← the set contains data point u;

11    if E_min[s] = ∅ or δ(u, v) < weight of E_min[s] then

12    E_min[s] ← (u, v);

13    for each set s contains any data point u ∈ V do

14    (u, v) ← E_min[s];

15    if u and v are in different sets then

16    merge the sets containing u and v;

17    add edge (u, v) into T with weight δ(u, v);

18 return T;
```

```txt
Algorithm 5: WindowOrder (G, y0)

Input : a proximity graph G = (V, E) of X and the entry point y0 of the proximity graph of Y
Output : the window order list

1 sort edges (xi, y0) with ascending order of δ(xi, y0) for each xi ∈ V, and denote them as e1, e2, · · · , e|X|;
2 i ← 1; T ← ∅;
3 assign each data point u ∈ V ∪ {y0} to the set only contains itself;
4 for each edge (p, q) in G's MST in ascending order of δ(p, q) do
5    while i ≤ |V| and weight of edge ei = (u, v) ≤ δ(p, q) do
6    if u and v are in different sets then
7    merge the sets containing u and v;
8    add edge (u, v) into T;
9    i ← i + 1;
10    if p and q are in different sets then
11    merge the sets containing p and q;
12    add edge (p, q) into T;
13 use DFS to derive parent node p[xi] for each xi ∈ V in T rooted at y0;
14 return (p[xi], xi) for each xi ∈ X according to the DFS order;
```

